				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6391595&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605926137" alt="Quadraginta" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6391595)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/quadraginta" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6391595); return false;" >Quadraginta</a></span> <span class="odate time_1605813757 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Nov 2020 19:22</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4822799">
									

<p>Heyo! I'll come and crit your draft you. Keep in mind I'm not clincal tone pro, so this'll largely be a 'what I think in general' type crit.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Let's&nbsp;do&nbsp;it!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is located at what was previously known as █████ Inc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Generally, redacting/blackboxing something in the ConProcs (Special Containment Procedures) isn't a good idea. Realistically speaking, redacting information in the ConProcs doesn't make sense in-universe. These are the procedures—made as simple and basic as possible for personnel—for containing something. Redacting part of the information would just make the job more difficult than it needs to be.</p>
<p>Also, even out of ConProcs, I don't really see a reason to redact the name of a location. In universe, you're being granted clearance to read a classified article. Knowing where it came from usually isn't going to be that bad.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and administered Class A Amnestic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Put 'a' between 'administered' and 'class'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following the unauthorized use of SCP-XXXX, no personnel under level four clearance are allowed in the same building as SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Probably a personal thing, but 'below level four clearance' sounds better than 'under'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>elevator shaft.The</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Put a space between 'shaft.' and 'The'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The elevator itself is comprised of steel and has seven buttons on the inside of it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remove 'itself'. Intuition says it's a tad unclinical to put it there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX functions as a normal elevator but when button seven is pressed the person(s) inside the elevator will be taken to the place they most desire to go.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'most desire to go' sounds off.<br />
Let's try, 'SCP-XXXX functions as a normal elevator, but when the seventh button is pressed, the person(s) inside will be transported to a location they subconsciously desire.'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Inside is a GPS that records the places it has gone.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Let's try, 'Inside is a GPS that keeps records of previously traversed locations.'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The elevator will not work without it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'The elevator will not function without the GPS.'<br />
That said, I'm not sure how to feel about this line. I assume the GPS is just planted in the elevator. Also, this implies that the personnel have tried&#8230;removing it? I don't see why they'd do that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Places recorded have been The Fiji Islands, low Earth orbit, and England.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you can merge this with an earlier sentence to turn it into:<br />
'Inside is a GPS that keeps records of previously explored locations, examples including The Fiji Islands, low Earth orbit, and England.'</p>
<p>It's cleaner and smoother.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In all instances the elevator returns within 20 minutes after leaving the facility area.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Puts a comma after 'instances'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Always talked of &quot;the fields&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hmmm you can change 'of' to 'about' and it would be better, but it still sounds unclinical.</p>
<p>Maybe, 'Frequently mentioned a location he referred to as, 'the fields.'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>personnel.Camera feed was choppy but the surroundings were normal.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Space after 'personnel.'</p>
<p>You should <strong>bold</strong> out 'Dr. Spencer's Diary' whenever you write down the entries. It makes it stand out and look cleaner.</p>
<p>'Foundation' should be capitalized because it's a proper noun.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The elevators GPS showed the word unkown on the location.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>'GPS displayed 'unknown' as the location.'</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Results: D-7586 appeared to have died of asphyxiation, presumably from being subjected to outer space.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't really like the way this is worded. 'Appeared to have died of asphyxiation, presumably from being subjected to outer space.'</p>
<p>You could just say that D-7586 died from exposure to outer space. It's been stated plainly thus far that the elevator takes you to where you want to go.</p>
<p>'Presume: suppose that something is the case on the basis of probability.'</p>
<p>There really isn't much probability here.</p>
<p>Apologies if this part sounds kinda overly nitpicky.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-4576 was a surfer. She lived on the beach and says that is where her heart is. D-4576 is very kind to all personnel considering the situation she is in.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Clinical tone is meant to portray something as emotionlessly as possible. This passage reads a little too much like normal prose.<br />
We could change it to, 'D-4576 once had a significant interest in surfing. She lived on a beach prior to becoming D-Class. She has been cooperative thus far.'</p>
<p>Even then, I'm not sure mentioning the cooperative part is necessary. Unless she shown shown be to uncooperative beforehand.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When D-4576 arrived back at the facility she was covered in sand. She stated it was the best day she has had in forever. Her dopamine levels were extremely high.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Second sentence is too emotional. Adjectives like 'extremely' are usually non-clinical.</p>
<p>Test 4 reads&#8230;super awkwardly. As well as the diary entry beforehand.</p>
<p>Also, the reference to SCP-682 wasn't a good move because references/cross-links nowadays are frowned upon.</p>
<p>This was a strange piece to critique, considering this is a greenlit idea with a draft from over a year ago. I looked at the edit history and see that it was updated a few days ago, yes, but it still feels&#8230;strange. I'll detail this more in my final thoughts collapsible.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+Final&nbsp;Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-Final&nbsp;Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>All in all, I'm not sure how to feel about this. 'Get in thing and go to desired/weird locale at push of button/drop of hat' isn't anything new, but it's always the execution that matters more than the concept. Everything has already been done in storytelling history, it's about what you do with a thing to differentiate it that makes it special.</p>
<p>But from what I've seen of your current draft, it's really standard with sub-clinical tone, bizarre and not really believable diary entries and a plot that essentially equates to 'these test logs freak me out and I'm afraid I might die. GG, fuck this.'</p>
<p>I should let you know that I, myself, am still pretty new here and I wasn't here in 2019; I joined a few months ago as of me writing this down. From what I've heard and seen, generic testing logs with little information aren't really good anymore.</p>
<p>I've heard one person say that testing logs aren't really good anymore, period, but like I think you could make this SCP work if you&#8230;say&#8230;</p>
<p>Did more in depth logs with video logs and such. Strap a video cam to some D-Class, have them go through some stuff and extend each test log to being a mini-adventure. Pretty standard stuff, but there's always an audience for that.</p>
<p>Also, you'll need to incorporate the researcher more organically into the story rather than have them write some extremely barebones diary entries.</p>
<p>Maybe they could maintain communication with the test D-Class subjects during test logs?</p>
<p>From what I've seen of your current logs, they essentially stick D-class in the thing, wave goodbye and tell them to be back before dinner. That doesn't really make sense considering the D-Class can just leave and not come back.</p>
<p>All in all, I hope everything I wrote made sense and I hope I helped. Good luck with this piece. I hope you continue pursuing this because it's nice to contribute to a lovely site like this ^_^</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

